<h2>How the Meeting-to-Opportunity Conversion Calculator works</h2><p>Meeting-to-opportunity conversion is opportunities created ÷ completed qualified meetings. It isolates the part of the funnel where discovery quality, qualification and next-step alignment turn conversations into pipeline. The calculator also back-solves the number of meetings needed for a target opportunity count at the current conversion rate.</p><h2>How to use this meeting-to-opportunity conversion calculator</h2><p>Enter completed meetings using a documented definition—for example, discovery meetings that actually occurred—and the opportunities created from those meetings. If your CRM allows one meeting to create multiple opportunities or an opportunity to originate without a meeting, decide how those exceptions are handled before comparing teams.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>A low rate can indicate targeting problems, weak qualification before the meeting, discovery execution or a very strict opportunity definition. A high rate is not automatically good if reps create low-quality opportunities too early. Pair the rate with downstream win rate and pipeline quality rather than optimizing the conversion percentage in isolation.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>Calendar bookings are not the same as completed meetings because no-shows and cancellations change the denominator. Timing also matters: opportunities may be created days after a meeting. Use a consistent attribution window and avoid comparing teams that use different stage-entry criteria.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>If 36 opportunities are created from 120 completed meetings, conversion is 30%. To create 50 opportunities at the same rate, the team needs about 167 comparable meetings. A manager can then decide whether the gap should be solved with more activity or better conversion.</p>
